{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S8zcimN2wq4syHRf7y3yi8","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental NMR Murday Cotts temperature dependence\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"link.springer.com\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553\"},{\"title\":\"r m cotts 1Z8Qzy\",\"url\":\"https://scite.ai/authors/r-m-cotts-1Z8Qzy\"},{\"title\":\"Self-diffusion coefficients of ionic liquids measured by multinuclear benchtop NMR\",\"url\":\"https://magritek.com/?p=21016\"},{\"title\":\"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/\"},{\"title\":\"s00231 011 0874 9\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9\"},{\"title\":\"subject:Self diffusion coefficients\",\"url\":\"https://fitforthem.unipa.it/subject:Self-diffusion coefficients\"},{\"title\":\"link.aps.org\",\"url\":\"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302\"},{\"title\":\"MIT Libraries logoDSpace@MIT\",\"url\":\"https://dspace.mit.edu/handle/1721.1/95617\"},{\"title\":\"Study of transport processes in Li-ion electrolytes by Multi-nuclear diffusion NMR\",\"url\":\"https://magritek.com/2020/09/21/study-of-transport-processes-in-li-ion-electrolytes-by-multi-nuclear-diffusion-nmr/\"},{\"title\":\"next.sciencegate.app\",\"url\":\"https://next.sciencegate.app/document/10.1557/opl.2012.1517\"}]\n\nThe search didn't return the Murday and Cotts paper itself, so I can't give its fitted temperature dependence. Here is what the results do show:\n\n- **The original reference:** A 2012 paper on liquid lithium lists \"Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium\" in *J Chem Phys* 48 (Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945).\n- **Their NMR method:** A later Cotts-group abstract describes measuring the coefficient of self-diffusion of 7Li in liquid lithium with nuclear magnetic resonance, using the spin-echo response to a pulsed field gradient (the diffusion coefficient was determined from observations of the dependence of the spin-echo signal upon the amplitude of a pulsed magnetic field gradient). That abstract is for a different paper, so I can't confirm it is the 1968 work.\n- **Agreement with simulation:** A 2012 molecular-dynamics study reports that calculated self-diffusion coefficient is in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere, and that the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature increasing. It also notes that little liquid lithium experimental self-diffusion coefficient were reported in the literature.\n- **Solid-lithium context:** A Springer paper on 7Li relaxation in solid lithium gives an Arrhenius-type fit with a prefactor of 0.038 cm²/s and an activation energy of 12.0 kcal/mol, but this is for the solid phase, not the liquid. Its reference list includes the Murday–Cotts 1971 *Z. Naturforsch.* paper.\n\nFor the actual Murday–Cotts liquid-lithium coefficients and their temperature fit, the next step would be to search for the 1968 *J. Chem. Phys.* article directly or for a review that tabulates liquid lithium self-diffusion data.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"484f894c-a7f8-4c52-9010-fb2d587eb7f8","uuid":"26e94ffa-a0d4-470c-ae84-fe877cf5137a","timestamp":"2026-11-01T08:42:45.435Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient experimental NMR Murday Cotts temperature dependence","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Jb8hE9cWbF57b1seH5zL7w","content":[{"title":"link.springer.com","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00883553"},{"title":"r m cotts 1Z8Qzy","url":"https://scite.ai/authors/r-m-cotts-1Z8Qzy"},{"title":"Self-diffusion coefficients of ionic liquids measured by multinuclear benchtop NMR","url":"https://magritek.com/?p=21016"},{"title":"self diffusion coefficient study of liquid lithium","url":"https://scholar.xjtu.edu.cn/en/publications/self-diffusion-coefficient-study-of-liquid-lithium/"},{"title":"s00231 011 0874 9","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00231-011-0874-9"},{"title":"subject:Self diffusion coefficients","url":"https://fitforthem.unipa.it/subject:Self-diffusion coefficients"},{"title":"link.aps.org","url":"https://link.aps.org/doi/10.1103/PhysRevB.83.094302"},{"title":"MIT Libraries logoDSpace@MIT","url":"https://dspace.mit.edu/handle/1721.1/95617"},{"title":"Study of transport processes in Li-ion electrolytes by Multi-nuclear diffusion NMR","url":"https://magritek.com/2020/09/21/study-of-transport-processes-in-li-ion-electrolytes-by-multi-nuclear-diffusion-nmr/"},{"title":"next.sciencegate.app","url":"https://next.sciencegate.app/document/10.1557/opl.2012.1517"}]},"The search didn't return the Murday and Cotts paper itself, so I can't give its fitted temperature dependence. Here is what the results do show:\n\n- **The original reference:** A 2012 paper on liquid lithium lists \"Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium\" in *J Chem Phys* 48 (Murday JS, Cotts RM (1968) Self-diffusion coefficient of liquid lithium. J Chem Phys 48:4938–4945).\n- **Their NMR method:** A later Cotts-group abstract describes measuring the coefficient of self-diffusion of 7Li in liquid lithium with nuclear magnetic resonance, using the spin-echo response to a pulsed field gradient (the diffusion coefficient was determined from observations of the dependence of the spin-echo signal upon the amplitude of a pulsed magnetic field gradient). That abstract is for a different paper, so I can't confirm it is the 1968 work.\n- **Agreement with simulation:** A 2012 molecular-dynamics study reports that calculated self-diffusion coefficient is in good agreement with Murday's experiment results in atmosphere, and that the liquid lithium self-diffusion coefficient increases with temperature increasing. It also notes that little liquid lithium experimental self-diffusion coefficient were reported in the literature.\n- **Solid-lithium context:** A Springer paper on 7Li relaxation in solid lithium gives an Arrhenius-type fit with a prefactor of 0.038 cm²/s and an activation energy of 12.0 kcal/mol, but this is for the solid phase, not the liquid. Its reference list includes the Murday–Cotts 1971 *Z. Naturforsch.* paper.\n\nFor the actual Murday–Cotts liquid-lithium coefficients and their temperature fit, the next step would be to search for the 1968 *J. Chem. Phys.* article directly or for a review that tabulates liquid lithium self-diffusion data."],"durationSeconds":6.090400312000002,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01S8zcimN2wq4syHRf7y3yi8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U3ib8crCKvN9dbURRytK6R","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"liquid lithium density temperature correlation g/cm3 Davison OR Ohse lithium handbook\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Yamdb: easily accessible thermophysical properties of liquid metals and molten salts\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.10922\"},{\"title\":\"ntrs.nasa.gov\",\"url\":\"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Contents - Index\",\"url\":\"https://fchartsoftware.com/ees/libr_help/hs400.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Lithium chloride · LiCl\",\"url\":\"https://processconvert.pages.dev/substances/lithium-chloride\"},{\"title\":\"Operational conditions:\",\"url\":\"https://www.chemeo.com/mid/22-277-a/Choline-chloride_Urea_lithium-chloride\"},{\"title\":\"ntrs.nasa.gov\",\"url\":\"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19680018893\"},{\"title\":\"core.ac.uk\",\"url\":\"https://core.ac.uk/download/132578668.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"unt.edu\",\"url\":\"https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1446580/m2/1/high_res_d/7152636.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"wulixb.iphy.ac.cn\",\"url\":\"https://wulixb.iphy.ac.cn/article/doi/10.7498/aps.55.76\"},{\"title\":\"core.ac.uk\",\"url\":\"https://core.ac.uk/works/150069412\"}]\n\nI didn't find the Davison or Ohse lithium density correlation coefficients directly, but two sources give useful partial results.\n\n- **Yamdb (arXiv 2402.10922):** This library lists a liquid lithium density of about 483 kg/m³ at 800 K, attributed to Ohse (1985). That is roughly 0.483 g/cm³. The table lists density at 800 K from Ohse (1985), alongside other properties from different sources.\n- **NASA NTRS report (19680018893):** This 1968 compilation fits lithium properties as functions of temperature and extrapolates them to about 1600 K. The Linear form of the polynomial was selected to represent the density. The text in this scan is badly garbled, but it appears to give a linear form of roughly 0.555 − 0.934×10⁻⁴·T g/cm³. That is my reading of the OCR, not a verified equation. As a check, it gives about 0.48 g/cm³ at 800 K, which agrees with the Ohse value above.\n- **core.ac.uk heat-pipe data sheets (132578668):** These sheets cover lithium and other liquid metals and rely on the Liquid Metals Handbook among other references. The OCR for the density entry is also garbled.\n\nI did not find the Davison correlation itself. To confirm the coefficients, check the original NASA report or the Liquid Metals Handbook directly.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"484f894c-a7f8-4c52-9010-fb2d587eb7f8","uuid":"4d95e2a3-dd73-4b97-9f6d-55d6b9fa03ed","timestamp":"2026-11-01T08:42:46.816Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"liquid lithium density temperature correlation g/cm3 Davison OR Ohse lithium handbook","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01HVQ3kbMLXeFPYahhsHj9ct","content":[{"title":"Yamdb: easily accessible thermophysical properties of liquid metals and molten salts","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.10922"},{"title":"ntrs.nasa.gov","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/19680018893/downloads/19680018893.pdf"},{"title":"Contents - Index","url":"https://fchartsoftware.com/ees/libr_help/hs400.htm"},{"title":"Lithium chloride · LiCl","url":"https://processconvert.pages.dev/substances/lithium-chloride"},{"title":"Operational conditions:","url":"https://www.chemeo.com/mid/22-277-a/Choline-chloride_Urea_lithium-chloride"},{"title":"ntrs.nasa.gov","url":"https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19680018893"},{"title":"core.ac.uk","url":"https://core.ac.uk/download/132578668.pdf"},{"title":"unt.edu","url":"https://digital.library.unt.edu/ark:/67531/metadc1446580/m2/1/high_res_d/7152636.pdf"},{"title":"wulixb.iphy.ac.cn","url":"https://wulixb.iphy.ac.cn/article/doi/10.7498/aps.55.76"},{"title":"core.ac.uk","url":"https://core.ac.uk/works/150069412"}]},"I didn't find the Davison or Ohse lithium density correlation coefficients directly, but two sources give useful partial results.\n\n- **Yamdb (arXiv 2402.10922):** This library lists a liquid lithium density of about 483 kg/m³ at 800 K, attributed to Ohse (1985). That is roughly 0.483 g/cm³. The table lists density at 800 K from Ohse (1985), alongside other properties from different sources.\n- **NASA NTRS report (19680018893):** This 1968 compilation fits lithium properties as functions of temperature and extrapolates them to about 1600 K. The Linear form of the polynomial was selected to represent the density. The text in this scan is badly garbled, but it appears to give a linear form of roughly 0.555 − 0.934×10⁻⁴·T g/cm³. That is my reading of the OCR, not a verified equation. As a check, it gives about 0.48 g/cm³ at 800 K, which agrees with the Ohse value above.\n- **core.ac.uk heat-pipe data sheets (132578668):** These sheets cover lithium and other liquid metals and rely on the Liquid Metals Handbook among other references. The OCR for the density entry is also garbled.\n\nI did not find the Davison correlation itself. To confirm the coefficients, check the original NASA report or the Liquid Metals Handbook directly."],"durationSeconds":6.735576658999999,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01U3ib8crCKvN9dbURRytK6R","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
